Subject: Say who the process is on its process pipe

The renderer's memory report listed each guest Vulkan device and each ColorBuffer by the
number the renderer gave its process, which says nothing about which app holds the memory.
After the process pipe's handshake the guest now writes one 64-byte word: the identity
request, its pid, and its name as /proc/self/cmdline starts (an app's package name). The
renderer answers nothing. A renderer that predates the word reads it as requests it does not
know and drops them, as it drops anything else it does not know there.

diff --git a/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp b/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
index 316723a3d..d04bd070a 100644
--- a/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
+++ b/guest/OpenglSystemCommon/HvSocketStream.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 
 #include <cutils/log.h>
 
+#include "dsa_graphics_memory.h"
 #include "dsa_spare_pages.h"
 
 HvSocketStream::HvSocketStream(size_t buffer_size, uint32_t port)
@@ -59,6 +60,14 @@ uint64_t HvSocketStream::processPipeInit() {
         ALOGW("HvSocketStream: process pipe initialisation failed");
         return 0;
     }
+    // Says who this process is, so the renderer's memory report names it rather than a
+    // number. A renderer that predates it reads the words as requests it does not know and
+    // drops them.
+    dsa::ProcessIdentity identity = {};
+    identity.op = dsa::kIdentifyRequest;
+    identity.pid = static_cast<uint32_t>(getpid());
+    dsa::readProcessName(identity.name, sizeof(identity.name));
+    writeFully(&identity, sizeof(identity));
     return process_uid;
 }
 
